WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.570
This sealed gas occupies two liters at one atmosphere.

2
00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:06.610
Its temperature stays at three hundred kelvin.

3
00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:09.000
What happens if we halve its volume?

4
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:10.930
The piston moves inward.

5
00:00:10.930 --> 00:00:14.630
The same amount of gas now occupies one liter,

6
00:00:14.630 --> 00:00:18.000
and its pressure rises to two atmospheres.

7
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.550
At constant temperature, the particles do not need to move faster.

8
00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:28.000
In the smaller space, their collisions with the walls produce greater pressure.

9
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:32.080
Boyle’s law keeps pressure times volume constant.

10
00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:38.000
One atmosphere times two liters equals two atmospheres times one liter.

11
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:41.390
On this graph, pressure rises as volume falls.

12
00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:43.820
The curve is not a straight line.

13
00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:47.000
Double the volume, and the pressure halves.

14
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:48.850
Try the piston yourself.

15
00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:55.000
Keep temperature and amount fixed, and predict the pressure before revealing it.
